%% ConfiguredAuth.tla, design "b": one custom connection whose token comes from
%% a helper program (the planned `command` auth type). States are what a person
%% would notice; arrows are events. The chart is checked by TLC through the
%% model, not by itself: keep it in step with ConfiguredAuth.tla.
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> NoToken
    NoToken: No token yet
    Waiting: Waiting for the sign-in helper (one run for every waiting request)
    Holding: Token in hand
    Sending: Request sent with the token
    Expired: Token past the expiry its helper printed
    Replaying: Refused once (401); fx dropped the token and sends the request once more
    Done: Request answered
    FailedHelper: Request failed, and fx says the helper failed
    FailedRefused: Request failed, and fx says the server refused the token
    Cancelled: Request cancelled, helper stopped if nobody else waits

    NoToken --> Waiting: a request needs a token
    Expired --> Waiting: a request needs a token
    Holding --> Sending: a request needs a token
    Waiting --> Waiting: another request needs a token (it joins the run)
    Waiting --> Sending: the helper prints a token
    Waiting --> FailedHelper: the helper fails, or fx's deadline stops it
    Waiting --> Cancelled: you cancel the last waiting request
    Sending --> Done: the server answers
    Sending --> Holding: the token stays in memory for the next request
    Holding --> Expired: the token passes its expiry
    Sending --> Replaying: the server refuses the token (first time)
    Replaying --> Sending: another request already got a newer token
    Replaying --> Waiting: no usable token, so the replay waits on a helper run
    Replaying --> Cancelled: you cancel before the replay
    Sending --> FailedRefused: the server refuses the new token too
    Done --> [*]
    FailedHelper --> [*]
    FailedRefused --> [*]
    Cancelled --> [*]

    note right of Waiting
        Rule breaks under planted defects, each shortest from the start:
        no-single-flight: a second request starts a second helper (OneRunPerConnection)
        orphan-helper: you cancel the last waiting request, the helper keeps running (NoOrphanRun)
        no-deadline: the helper hangs and the request never ends (RequestSettles)
        silent-failure: the helper fails and fx gives no reason (FailureSaysSo)
    end note
    note right of Replaying
        refresh-loop: a second 401 replays again (RefreshCapped)
        resend-same: the refused token is sent again (ResendHasNewToken)
    end note
    note left of Expired
        send-expired: a request sends the expired token (NoExpiredTokenSent)
        shared-cache: a request on c2 sends c1's token (TokenStaysWithItsConnection)
    end note
